  <h3>Detailed Findings</h3>
  <ul class="findings">
    <li><strong>MCQs doubled:</strong> The MCQ section expanded from 15 questions (2015–2019) to 15 in 2021 and then to 15 from 2021–2025. However, the proportional share shifted because Short and Long counts also changed. MCQs consistently represent the largest share at roughly 45–60% of total questions.</li>
    <li><strong>Short questions expanded most:</strong> The most dramatic change is in Short questions — from 9–10 per year (2015–2019) to 15 per year (2021–2025), a 50–67% increase. This signals that the board placed greater emphasis on breadth of knowledge coverage.</li>
    <li><strong>Long questions remained stable:</strong> Long questions held at 3 per year throughout the entire period, making them the most structurally stable component of the exam.</li>
    <li><strong>Proportion shift:</strong> The Short question share rose from ~36–40% (pre-2021) to ~45% (post-2021), while the MCQ share correspondingly dipped from ~55–60% to ~45%.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Interpretation:</strong> The exam moved from an MCQ-heavy format toward a more balanced distribution. Educators should note that Short question preparation now demands proportionally more attention than it did in the 2015–2019 era.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- Section 4: Topic Patterns -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>4. Recurring Topics &amp; Question Content Patterns</h2>
  <p>Keyword and topic cluster analysis was performed on the question text column (after removing MCQ options and common stop words). This surfaces the concepts and themes that the BIEK examination consistently tests.</p>

  <div class="chart-wrap">
