      <thead>
        <tr><th>Topic Cluster</th>{''.join(f'<th>{y}</th>' for y in all_years_list)}<th>Total</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>{topic_table_rows()}</tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Key Topic Findings</h3>
  <ul class="findings">
    <li><strong>Database Fundamentals is the single most tested domain</strong>, appearing in questions every year with consistently high frequency. Keywords like "database", "DBMS", "record", "field", and "tuple" are among the top 10 most frequent.</li>
    <li><strong>Loops &amp; Iteration</strong> is the most tested programming concept, with "loop", "while", and "for" appearing regularly. Nested loops are a recurring sub-theme.</li>
    <li><strong>Operators</strong> (arithmetic, logical, relational, increment/decrement) are heavily tested, especially in MCQs. This is the most predictable MCQ topic.</li>
    <li><strong>Functions &amp; Recursion</strong> appears with high regularity, particularly in Long questions asking students to define, explain, or write recursive programs.</li>
    <li><strong>Database Keys &amp; Relationships</strong> (primary key, foreign key, candidate key, relationships) is a concentrated sub-domain that appears almost every year.</li>
    <li><strong>MS Access</strong> questions (tables, queries, forms, reports) are a staple of the exam, typically appearing as MCQs and occasionally in Long questions.</li>
    <li><strong>Data Types &amp; Variables</strong> and <strong>I/O &amp; Format Specifiers</strong> are foundational topics that appear most frequently in MCQs.</li>
    <li><strong>Post-2021 evolution:</strong> Newer exams introduced questions on structures, pointers, arrays, bitwise operators, and ER diagrams — topics that were absent or rare before 2021. This suggests a curriculum update or exam modernization.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- Section 5: Strategic Insights -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>5. Strategic Insights for Exam Preparation</h2>
  <p>Based on the data patterns identified above, the following actionable recommendations are offered for educators and students.</p>

  <h3>5.1 High-Priority Topics (Tested Nearly Every Year)</h3>
  <p>These topics have appeared in 8 or more of the 10 available examination years. Students who master these cover the majority of likely exam content:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Database fundamentals</strong> (DBMS, RDBMS, records, fields, tuples, attributes) — the most consistently tested domain</li>
    <li><strong>Database keys</strong> (primary, foreign, candidate, composite) and <strong>relationships</strong> (one-to-one, one-to-many, many-to-many)</li>
    <li><strong>MS Access objects</strong> (tables, queries, forms, reports, macros)</li>
    <li><strong>Loops</strong> (for, while, do-while, nested loops) — both conceptual MCQs and program-writing Short/Long questions</li>
    <li><strong>Operators</strong> (arithmetic, relational, logical, increment/decrement) — an MCQ staple</li>
    <li><strong>Functions</strong> (prototypes, user-defined, recursion) — a reliable Long question topic</li>
    <li><strong>Format specifiers &amp; I/O functions</strong> (printf, scanf, getch, gets)</li>
    <li><strong>Data types</strong> (int, float, char, double, sizes)</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5.2 Question-Type Strategies</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>MCQs (15 marks):</strong> Focus on definitions, syntax rules, operator behavior, data type sizes, and MS Access object functions. MCQs test recall precision — common traps include confusing <code>=</code> with <code>==</code>, mixing format specifiers, and misidentifying database terminology (tuple vs. attribute).</li>
    <li><strong>Short Questions (15 marks post-2021):</strong> Prepare concise definitions, small programs (area calculations, table printing, number reversal), and concept comparisons (while vs. do-while, compiler vs. interpreter). The expanded Short section means more breadth is required.</li>
    <li><strong>Long Questions (3 marks):</strong> Practice extended essay responses on: (1) loop structures with program examples, (2) function types and recursion, (3) database models and MS Access objects. These three areas account for the vast majority of Long questions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5.3 Adapting to the Post-2021 Format</h3>
  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Format shift:</strong> The exam expanded from ~25 to 33 questions starting 2021. The increase came almost entirely from Short questions (9–10 → 15). Educators should adjust mock exams and practice sessions to reflect this larger Short question section.
  </div>
  <ul>
    <li>Allocate more preparation time to <strong>Short question breadth</strong> — the expanded section covers a wider range of sub-topics.</li>
    <li>Note the emergence of <strong>newer topics</strong> post-2021: structures, pointers, arrays, bitwise operators, ER diagrams, binary search, Fibonacci series, and ACID properties. While not yet dominant, these signal a modernization trend.</li>
    <li>The <strong>Long question pattern</strong> (3 questions covering C programming, database theory, and functions/structures) has remained remarkably consistent — this is the most predictable section.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>5.4 Curriculum Alignment Recommendations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Weight database topics more heavily in teaching time</strong> — they account for approximately 30–35% of all questions across the decade.</li>
    <li><strong>Integrate programming practice into every unit</strong> — program-writing questions appear in both Short and Long sections and span loops, functions, arrays, and file handling.</li>
    <li><strong>Ensure MS Access coverage</strong> — despite being a relatively narrow tool, it receives disproportionate exam attention due to its role in the BIEK syllabus.</li>
    <li><strong>Introduce advanced C concepts earlier</strong> — the post-2021 trend toward pointers, structures, and file handling suggests these will become more prominent, not less.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- Section 6: Conclusions -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>6. Conclusions &amp; Limitations</h2>

  <h3>Principal Conclusions</h3>
  <ul class="findings">
    <li><strong>The BIEK Computer Science exam has undergone a meaningful structural shift.</strong> The pre-2021 format (~25 questions, MCQ-heavy) and the post-2021 format (33 questions, more balanced) represent two distinct eras. This is not a stable exam — educators must prepare students for the current format, not historical averages.</li>
    <li><strong>Database topics dominate the content landscape.</strong> Across all question types and years, database/DBMS/MS-Access content consistently accounts for roughly one-third of the exam. This is the single most important domain for exam preparation.</li>
    <li><strong>C programming fundamentals remain central</strong> — loops, functions, operators, and data types are tested every year without exception.</li>
    <li><strong>The exam is modernizing.</strong> Newer topics (pointers, structures, file handling, ER diagrams, ACID properties) are appearing post-2021, suggesting the board is updating the syllabus to reflect contemporary CS education standards.</li>
    <li><strong>Long questions are highly predictable.</strong> They consistently follow a three-question pattern covering: (1) control structures/loops, (2) functions/data types/I-O, and (3) database models/DBMS concepts.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Limitations</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>2020 data is missing.</strong> The dataset contains no questions from 2020, likely due to COVID-19 examination disruptions. This gap prevents precise identification of when the format transition occurred.</li>
    <li><strong>No distinction between annual and supplementary papers.</strong> Some years may include questions from both examination sessions, which could inflate counts for those years. The dataset does not encode this distinction.</li>
    <li><strong>Small sample size.</strong> With 327 total questions across 10 years, fine-grained statistical analysis (e.g., topic-level trends within specific question types) has limited power.</li>
    <li><strong>Community-compiled data.</strong> This is a non-official dataset; minor transcription errors or omissions may exist.</li>
    <li><strong>Keyword-based topic mapping</strong> is approximate. Some questions span multiple topics, and the mapping relies on keyword presence rather than semantic understanding.</li>
    <li><strong>The <code>$</code> delimiter</strong> in the question column required special parsing. MCQ options and Long question sub-parts were separated for keyword analysis but may still introduce noise.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout danger">
    <strong>Data Integrity:</strong> All figures, percentages, and trends in this report are derived exclusively from the provided <code>data.csv</code>. No statistics have been fabricated or supplemented from external sources. Where data is absent or ambiguous (notably 2020), this has been explicitly noted.
  </div>
</div>

</div>

<div class="report-footer">
