he largest section by count.</strong> At 15 questions (Phase 3), they equal MCQs in volume. In Phase 2, they were actually the majority at {short_pct_2017}% of total questions.</li>", "+    <li><strong>Long questions are structurally invariant</strong> at 3 per year ({yearly_type[yearly_type['year']==2021]['Long_pct'].values[0]}% of the exam in Phase 3). They are the most predictable component.</li>", "+    <li><strong>The exam has moved from MCQ-dominated toward a balanced format.</strong> Phase 1 was MCQ-heavy ({mcq_pct_2015}% MCQ vs. {short_pct_2015}% Short). Phase 3 is nearly balanced ({mcq_pct_2021}% MCQ vs. {short_pct_2021}% Short).</li>", "   </ul>", " ", "   <div class="callout">", "-    <strong>Interpretation:</strong> The exam moved from an MCQ-heavy format toward a more balanced distribution. Educators should note that Short question preparation now demands proportionally more attention than it did in the 2015–2019 era.", "+    <strong>Interpretation:</strong> The exam evolved from a recall-heavy, MCQ-dominated format toward one that balances recall with broader knowledge coverage. Educators should note that Short question preparation now demands equal attention to MCQ preparation — a shift from the 2015–2016 era where MCQs dominated.", "   </div>", " </div>", " "]}, {"oldStart": 668, "oldLines": 19, "newStart": 716, "newLines": 19, "lines": [" ", "   <h3>5.2 Question-Type Strategies</h3>", "   <ul>", "-    <li><strong>MCQs (15 marks):</strong> Focus on definitions, syntax rules, operator behavior, data type sizes, and MS Access object functions. MCQs test recall precision — common traps include confusing <code>=</code> with <code>==</code>, mixing format specifiers, and misidentifying database terminology (tuple vs. attribute).</li>", "-    <li><strong>Short Questions (15 marks post-2021):</strong> Prepare concise definitions, small programs (area calculations, table printing, number reversal), and concept comparisons (while vs. do-while, compiler vs. in