  <h3>Three-Phase Exam Evolution</h3>
  <div class="phase-timeline">
    <div class="phase-card phase1">
      <h4>Phase 1: 2015–2016</h4>
      <p><strong>27 questions</strong> per year (15 MCQs, 9 Short, 3 Long)</p>
      <p>MCQ-dominated format with a smaller Short question section.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-card phase2">
      <h4>Phase 2: 2017–2019</h4>
      <p><strong>36 questions</strong> per year (15 MCQs, 18 Short, 3 Long)</p>
      <p>Short questions doubled from 9 to 18 — the single largest structural change in the dataset.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="phase-card phase3">
      <h4>Phase 3: 2021–2025</h4>
      <p><strong>33 questions</strong> per year (15 MCQs, 15 Short, 3 Long)</p>
      <p>Short questions contracted from 18 to 15. Post-pandemic format settling at a middle ground.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Key Observations</h3>
  <ul class="findings">
    <li><strong>Largest shift in 2017:</strong> Total questions jumped from 27 to 36 (+33.3%), driven entirely by Short questions doubling from 9 to 18. MCQ and Long question counts remained unchanged.</li>
    <li><strong>Post-pandemic contraction (2021):</strong> Total questions dropped from 36 to 33 (−8.3%), as Short questions decreased from 18 to 15 while MCQs and Long questions stayed at 15 and 3 respectively.</li>
    <li><strong>MCQs are the most stable component</strong> at exactly 15 questions every single year from 2015 to 2025.</li>
    <li><strong>Long questions are equally stable</strong> at exactly 3 per year throughout the entire period.</li>
    <li><strong>Short questions are the sole variable</strong> — they are the only component that has changed, making them the key driver of exam volume shifts.</li>
    <li><strong>2020 gap:</strong> The absence of 2020 data means the exact transition point between the 36-question and 33-question formats cannot be pinpointed.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<!-- Section 3: Question Type Composition -->
