  <h3>Detailed Findings</h3>
  <ul class="findings">
    <li><strong>MCQ share declined as Short questions expanded.</strong> In Phase 1, MCQs comprised {mcq_pct_2015}% of the exam; by Phase 2 this fell to {mcq_pct_2017}%, and in Phase 3 to {mcq_pct_2021}%. The absolute MCQ count never changed (15), but their proportional weight dropped as Short questions grew.</li>
    <li><strong>Short questions are now the largest section by count.</strong> At 15 questions (Phase 3), they equal MCQs in volume. In Phase 2, they were actually the majority at {short_pct_2017}% of total questions.</li>
    <li><strong>Long questions are structurally invariant</strong> at 3 per year ({yearly_type[yearly_type['year']==2021]['Long_pct'].values[0]}% of the exam in Phase 3). They are the most predictable component.</li>
    <li><strong>The exam has moved from MCQ-dominated toward a balanced format.</strong> Phase 1 was MCQ-heavy ({mcq_pct_2015}% MCQ vs. {short_pct_2015}% Short). Phase 3 is nearly balanced ({mcq_pct_2021}% MCQ vs. {short_pct_2021}% Short).</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Interpretation:</strong> The exam evolved from a recall-heavy, MCQ-dominated format toward one that balances recall with broader knowledge coverage. Educators should note that Short question preparation now demands equal attention to MCQ preparation — a shift from the 2015–2016 era where MCQs dominated.
